/-!
# Criteria for étaleness at a point

For a morphism of schemes locally of finite presentation, étaleness at a point is equivalent to
flatness of the induced map of local rings together with étaleness or unramifiedness of the
scheme-theoretic fibre, with vanishing of the relative differentials or of either of their two
canonically identified residue-field fibres, or with the condition that the maximal ideal of the
base generates the maximal ideal of the source and that the residue field extension is finite
separable. It is equivalent also to the existence of an affine neighbourhood on which the
morphism is standard smooth of relative dimension zero, has a square presentation whose Jacobian
determinant does not vanish at the point, is standard étale, or has an explicit presentation
`R[x]_Q/(P)` with `P` monic whose derivative does not vanish at the point.

## Main results

* `AlgebraicGeometry.etaleAt_tfae` gives the ten characterizations of étaleness at a point.

## Implementation notes

The entries of `List.TFAE` follow the source's order, except that the two canonically identified
cotangent spaces of the source's condition (5) are separate entries, so that either one alone
gives the criterion. Their canonical identification is
`Stacks.FiberCotangentAssembly.fiberDifferentialsTensorIdentification`. Conditions (4) and (5)
use `Stacks.SmoothAtJacobian.relativeDifferentialStalk`, the actual stalk of the sheaf of
relative differentials, and its residue-field fibres.

In the source's condition (7) a standard smooth algebra of relative dimension zero records a
square presentation with invertible Jacobian; condition (8) instead asks only that the Jacobian
avoid the prime corresponding to the point, and shrinking to the basic open it cuts out passes
between the two. In condition (10) the presentation is an isomorphism of the coordinate ring with
a localization of a monic one-variable polynomial quotient, over the target coordinate ring, and
the derivative again only avoids the point's prime.

Flatness of the induced map of local rings is flatness of
`AlgebraicGeometry.Scheme.Hom.stalkMap`. Unramifiedness of the fibre is read at
`AlgebraicGeometry.Scheme.Hom.asFiber`, the point of the scheme-theoretic fibre determined by the
given point, and transfers to unramifiedness of the morphism itself by
`AlgebraicGeometry.unramifiedAt_iff_of_isPullback` applied to the cartesian square defining the
fibre.

## References

* [Stacks Project, Tag 01V9](https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/01V9)
* [Stacks Project, Tag 02GF](https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/02GF)
* [Stacks Project, Tag 02GU](https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/02GU)
-/

@[expose] public section

